# zscan.py is executed on Linux. A Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true (the
# Git-for-Windows default) rewrites it to CRLF, and the shebang line then ends
# in a carriage return:
#
#     $ ./zscan.py
#     env: 'python3\r': No such file or directory
#
# Forcing LF on checkout keeps the file runnable whichever OS it was cloned or
# downloaded on before being copied to the target host.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py  text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf

# zscan.ps1 is Windows-only, but PowerShell 5.1+ runs LF-terminated scripts
# without complaint, so it is kept LF for consistency with the rest of the repo
# and so a release asset is byte-identical to the committed file.
#
# Keep zscan.ps1 pure ASCII. Windows PowerShell 5.1 decodes a .ps1 without a BOM
# using the machine's ANSI codepage, not UTF-8, so a UTF-8 em dash (e2 80 94) or
# box-drawing rule (e2 94 80) is read as mojibake whose 0x94 byte becomes U+201D
# RIGHT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK -- a character the PowerShell parser accepts as a
# string delimiter. One such character inside a double-quoted string terminates
# it early and cascades into dozens of bogus syntax errors on an otherwise valid
# script. ASCII parses identically under every codepage, with or without a BOM.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

LICENSE text eol=lf
